WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.241
Secure serialization review should be concrete.

2
00:00:03.241 --> 00:00:20.344
Look for custom converters, raw object serialization, broad model binding, polymorphic settings, type metadata, legacy binary serializers, XML parser settings, caches, queues, import and export tools, diagnostic object dumps, and support utilities.

3
00:00:20.344 --> 00:00:25.654
These areas often hold the assumptions that ordinary feature review can miss.

4
00:00:25.654 --> 00:00:32.136
Dependency review matters because serializers and parsers are code that process boundary data.

5
00:00:32.136 --> 00:00:33.515
Review System.

6
00:00:33.515 --> 00:00:34.894
Text.

7
00:00:34.894 --> 00:00:45.377
Json options, XML libraries, document-processing components, messaging libraries, compatibility packages, cache serializers, and third-party converters.

8
00:00:45.377 --> 00:00:56.824
Ask whether the dependency is maintained, whether its configuration is understood, whether it processes untrusted data, and whether it has access to sensitive fields.

9
00:00:56.824 --> 00:01:09.444
Testing should include malformed input, oversized input, missing fields, unknown fields, incompatible versions, invalid states, authorization boundaries, and sensitive field exposure.

10
00:01:09.444 --> 00:01:13.099
These tests do not need unsafe examples to be useful.

11
00:01:13.099 --> 00:01:28.064
Defensive test data can be ordinary but unexpected: a missing ExampleTenantId, an unsupported message version, an empty required field, an overly large document, or a field that should never appear in an API response.

12
00:01:28.064 --> 00:01:30.891
Legacy remediation starts with inventory.

13
00:01:30.891 --> 00:01:43.304
Find where serialized data enters, where it leaves, where it is stored, which formats are used, who owns each contract, and which flows involve sensitive output or untrusted input.

14
00:01:43.304 --> 00:01:54.338
Prioritize the highest-risk paths first: external inputs, long-lived stored data, broad type handling, sensitive outputs, and components with unclear ownership.

15
00:01:54.338 --> 00:01:57.441
Remediation usually combines several changes.

16
00:01:57.441 --> 00:02:11.785
Introduce DTOs, narrow contracts, add validation, document ownership, replace unsafe or poorly understood serializers, configure parsers, reduce sensitive output, add compatibility tests, and plan migrations.

17
00:02:11.785 --> 00:02:18.681
Avoid trying to solve every legacy path in one large rewrite unless the organization can support it.

18
00:02:18.681 --> 00:02:22.129
Incremental, owned remediation often works better.

19
00:02:22.129 --> 00:02:26.957
Monitoring helps teams see whether serialization controls are working.

20
00:02:26.957 --> 00:02:38.749
Track deserialization failures, unsupported versions, message rejection patterns, import errors, repeated malformed input, and unusual message volume at a defensive level.

21
00:02:38.749 --> 00:02:48.128
These signals can reveal operational drift, broken clients, stale producers, or workflows that need better validation and documentation.
